WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.520
When you picture a superhero, who do you see?

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.000
Batman?

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.320
Superman?

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:08.940
Spiderman?

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:10.440
The Hulk?

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:18.160
Thanks to a growing collection of data, we
know that our cultural icons are not consistent

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.020
with what we look like as a country.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:25.720
This fact extends to virtually every form
of mass media in the States.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:33.240
Our best data suggests that the American population
is roughly equal in gender, and about 38%

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.000
are a minority of some sort.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:40.060
Which reveals a simple truth about the
latest Marvel superhero film:

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:46.160
People are excited about Black Panther because
the demand for media that centers minorities

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.780
is far greater than the available supply.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:56.680
This becomes obvious when you look at a series
of studies by UCLA’s school of sociology.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.660
Much of what we knew about diversity in Hollywood
was anecdotal.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.560
So we decided to design a report that would
be the gold standard in terms of looking at

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.400
the birds eye view of what’s happening and
tracking that over time.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:13.800
Roughly 1 out of every 10 major films are
directed by minorities.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.540
A similar percentage cast minorities in
their lead roles.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.860
And even fewer of these films are written
by minorities.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:30.100
This principle applies to nearly every facet
of media, including executives and talent agents.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:33.180
You can’t underestimate the role of the
gatekeepers.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.869
If you’re not on the roster, you’re going to have a hard time breaking in.

00:01:35.869 --> 00:01:39.860
Which of course has created obstacles for
people of color and women, in particular.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.820
Which means that the culture represented on
screen is determined by a sample that doesn’t

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.040
accurately represent the population.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:53.600
If you look at pop culture depictions of Africa,
you don’t ever get to see this place that’s

00:01:53.619 --> 00:01:55.369
supposed to be the future.

00:01:55.369 --> 00:02:00.320
Wakanda is almost like this rewriting of history
of what life would’ve been like.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:03.770
It’s this amazing fantasy that you never
get to see on screen.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:06.760
Black Panther is all about representation.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.719
It centers the story on women and people of
color.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.879
And that representation matters to young people. They're the future target audience.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:20.120
The minority share of the population was relatively
low in 1970.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:25.880
But today, nearly 40% of the US population
is in a minority group.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.060
And that percentage is only expected to increase.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:31.700
The genre doesn’t matter.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.440
Audiences want to see people like themselves
on the screen.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:40.260
If you have a good story, and the narrative
is compelling, and you have people of color

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.040
in it, then diverse audiences are probably
going to go see it.

